    {
      "id": "address-plurals",
      "number": "36",
      "heading": "The Plurals of Address",
      "statement": "Each second-person grade has its own plural: **তোরা**, **তোমরা**, **আপনারা**. Number sits in the pronoun; the finite verb ending stays the same as in the singular of that grade.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "তোরা",
          "roman": "tora",
          "gloss": "you (pl., very familiar)"
        },
        {
          "target": "তোমরা",
          "roman": "tomra",
          "gloss": "you (pl., familiar)"
        },
        {
          "target": "আপনারা",
          "roman": "apnara",
          "gloss": "you (pl., polite)"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [],
      "seeAlso": [
        "three-address"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "first-person",
      "number": "37",
      "heading": "The First Person",
      "statement": "**আমি** (ami) is 'I'; **আমরা** (amra) is 'we'. The verb takes one first-person ending for both; plurality is carried by the pronoun alone.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "আমি",
          "roman": "ami",
          "gloss": "I"
        },
        {
          "target": "আমরা",
          "roman": "amra",
          "gloss": "we"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "Beames notes that ami was historically a plural; আমরা is the later plural built on it."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [],
      "sources": [
